---

<Layout
  fullTitle="Shanghai Hairy Crab: Seasonal Delicacy Guide"
  description="Everything about Shanghai hairy crab (da zha xie): when to eat, how to eat, the best restaurants, and why this autumn delicacy is worth planning a trip around."
>
  <main>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 md:pt-28 pb-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none font-semibold text-slate-900">Shanghai Hairy Crab: Autumn's Greatest Luxury</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">Every autumn, Shanghai surrenders to an obsession: the hairy crab. These small freshwater crabs, with their sweet meat and decadent golden roe, inspire devotion, debate, and dining pilgrimages across the city.</p>
        <AuthorByline />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Makes Hairy Crab Special</h2>
        <p>The Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) gets its English nickname from the soft, fur-like setae on its claws. Native to the Yangtze River delta, these crabs are celebrated for two things: the sweet, delicate meat and the rich, creamy golden roe that gourmands consider one of the great luxuries of Chinese cuisine. The flavor is subtly sweet with an almost nutty undertone, completely unlike ocean crab varieties.</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">When Is Hairy Crab Season?</h2>
        <p>Hairy crab season runs from September through December, with the peak in October and November. Female crabs (with their prized roe) are at their best in October, while male crabs peak in November. The ninth lunar month for females and the tenth lunar month for males is the traditional rule of thumb, and serious diners plan their restaurant visits accordingly.</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">How to Eat Hairy Crab</h2>
        <p>Eating hairy crab is a ritual unto itself. The crabs are steamed whole and served with a dipping sauce of black vinegar, ginger, and sugar. Diners methodically disassemble the crab with specialized tools, starting with the claws, moving to the legs, then opening the shell to reach the prized roe. The roe is eaten first, savored slowly, before the rest of the crab is picked clean. It is a lengthy, meditative process that Shanghai locals approach with near-religious seriousness. The crab is considered a cooling (yin) food, so it is traditionally paired with warming Shaoxing wine and ginger tea.</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900">The Best Hairy Crab Restaurants</h2>
        <p>Xin Rong Ji is the premier destination for hairy crab in Shanghai, offering elaborate tasting menus that showcase the crab in multiple preparations. Cheng Long Hang, a specialist crab restaurant, provides a more focused experience at a lower price point. For the full luxury treatment, book a private room at YongFoo Elite, where the crab is paired with vintage Shaoxing wines.</p>
        <div class="mt-10">
          <a href="/" class="btn-soft bg-accent-600 hover:bg-accent-700 text-white text-sm font-medium px-6 py-3 transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2">Find Top Shanghai Restaurants</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <ArticleSchema
